success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**How to read the thresholds**\n\n- The **$0.01-per-share and 0.5%-of-NAV** tests are **industry conventions, not SEC rules**. They trace to 1990s SEC staff positions and a never-adopted 1997 proposal.\n- Many policies treat an error as material only if it meets **both** tests. Smaller errors are usually corrected going forward. Each fund's written policy controls, and some are stricter or cumulative.\n\n**What governs correction**\n\n- The **board-approved NAV error policy** under Rule 38a-1, plus Rule 2a-5 valuation oversight.\n- The **administration agreement**, which sets the standard of care, indemnification and who pays.\n- Prospectus disclosure and fiduciary and anti-fraud duties, with escalation to the CCO and board.\n\n**ETF-specific nuance**\n\n- Secondary-market investors trade at market prices, so harm falls mainly on **authorized participants' creations and redemptions** and the fund. The remedy is reprocessing AP transactions or reimbursing the fund.\n- Under Rule 6c-11, published NAV, premium/discount and holdings data should also be corrected.","ttft_ms":3785,"type":"result","duration_ms":6288,"uuid":"e0cd3b93-438f-46e3-854f-91e1692c50fc","ttft_stream_ms":477,"time_to_request_ms":55,"first_content_frame_ms":477,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
